Parser
------

The lexer and parser are written in F*:

* FStarC.Parser.Lexer.fst: the lexer, which produces the same tokens,
  positions and comments as the former sedlex lexer. It works directly
  on the UTF-8 input string (byte offsets,
  FStarC.String.byte_at/code_point_at), with offsets and characters as
  native ints (FStarC.SmallInt).
  FStarC.Parser.Unicode.fst holds the Unicode tables, generated by
  gen_unicode_tables.py.

* FStarC.Parser.Pratt.fst: parser state, combinators (peek/expect/accept,
  attempt, lookahead), Menhir-compatible range helpers, and an extensible
  Pratt engine. A grammar is a table of leading and trailing rules, keyed by
  token kind ("LPAREN") or kind and text ("IDENT:fn", for non-reserved
  keywords). Each rule has an explicit precedence; higher binds tighter.

* FStarC.Parser.Grammar.fst: the F* grammar (terms, patterns, binders,
  declarations) and the entry points parse_file, parse_incremental and
  parse_term. The table `fstar_grammar` lists all term-level rules.

* FStarC.Parser.Frontend.fst: the interface used by the rest of the
  compiler (and the IDE): reading files (and the IDE's virtual file
  system), parsing whole files, fragments, terms and #lang-* blocks,
  dispatching to extension languages (FStarC.Parser.AST.Util), and the
  parser for the arguments of --warn_error.

The printer in FStarC.Parser.ToDocument.fst should be kept up to date
with the grammar.

To benchmark, use --ext parser=bench: it times each file with lex (the
lexer only), parse (the parser only, on pre-lexed tokens) and new
(lexer + parser), printing "PARSER-BENCH <file> <name> <ms>" to stderr.
Options: --ext parser_bench_iters=N (default 10) and
--ext parser_bench_only=<name>. Benchmark a release build
(FSTAR_DUNE_RELEASE=1): the default dune profile compiles with -opaque,
which prevents cross-module inlining (e.g. of FStarC.SmallInt).

The parser is meant to produce exactly the same ASTs on base F* as the
former Menhir parser, since stage0 still parses the compiler with Menhir.
When it replaced Menhir, it did so on all of ulib, src, tests and
examples. Some Menhir quirks are emulated deliberately: ranges of productions starting with an empty
inlined option begin at the end of the previous token (eps_loc), and
gensym'd names are generated in Menhir's reduction order.

Term precedences (from loosest to tightest):

    0   ;  ;;-ops          1  <- (guarded)       5  <:  $:  (by ...)
    10  <==> (right)       20 ==> (right)        30 ->  (right)
    40  \/                 50 /\                 60 ,  (tuples)
    71  :=                 72 op0a |..           73 op0b &..
    74  op0c = <..         75 op0d $..           76 |>
    77  <| (right)         78 op1 @ ^ (right)    79 op2 + - (left)
    80  prefix - ` '       81 :: (right)         82 & (dep. tuples, right)
    83  op3L * / %         84 op3R ** (right)    85 `infix`
    86  op4 (right)        100 atoms, applications

The ctx record captures what precedences cannot: e.g. in the domains of
arrows inside type ascriptions, levels strictly between `->` and `=` are
disabled (tmArrow(tmNoEq) in the Menhir grammar).

To extend the grammar, build a new grammar from fstar_grammar with
add_leading/add_trailing and run the parser under run_with; rules for
the same key are tried in registration order.

Pulse
-----

pulse/src/syntax_extension/PulseSyntaxExtension.Grammar.fst is the Pulse
grammar, formerly pulseparser.mly. It provides the parse_decl,
parse_peek_id and parse_lang entry points used by
PulseSyntaxExtension.ASTBuilder; parse_lang leaves the comments in the
buffer of FStarC.Parser.AST.Util, from which Frontend collects them.

- Keywords are reserved per grammar: the Pulse lexer pass (start_with rewrite_token) turns
  identifiers such as `requires` or `mut` into keyword tokens, so that
  they terminate applications (`requires p ensures q`). F* code in a
  #lang-pulse block cannot use them as identifiers.
- pulse_grammar extends fstar_grammar with a leading rule for fn types
  (`fn (x:int) requires p ensures q`, optionally with ghost/atomic/...),
  so that they are available in all F* term positions of a Pulse file.
  They are accepted at a looser level than in Menhir (e.g. as the
  codomain of an arrow).
- Statements and declarations are ordinary recursive-descent functions
  that call into the F* term parser at the appropriate levels.
- Ranges reproduce the Menhir parser's, including the ones that start at the end
  of the previous token because of an empty non-inlined nonterminal
  (optional_norewrite, option(qual), an empty binder list).
- Syntax errors report the actual error instead of "Syntax error".
